  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Today will be a seasonable day with temperatures in most \nareas at least reaching 60 to the mid 60s. River valley fog will end \nearly this evening as the combination of some building high level \nclouds and increasing southeast to south flow will develop. A \nprefrontal trough will approach later this evening. It will try to \nproduce a few showers as moisture lifts north ahead of it. However, \nthere's very little to work with, and so perhaps a stray shower \nnorth of Route 11 in our area of responsibility could occur.\n\nThe actual frontal boundary will shift east beyond midnight through \nMonday night. There remains little change with regard to the strong \ndynamical forcing that will be present, but marginal moisture \nparameters. A few post-frontal showers could take place, especially \nalong the international border as the vort max associated with the \nupper trough arrives with sufficient moisture to still allow some \nactivity, particularly over northwest facing slopes. The bulk of the \ncold air will not arrive until well after the rain has exited, so \nthere will be limited, but not impossible, chance for a few \nflakes over our northern summits.\n\nThe air mass coming in for Monday is quite chilly. Brisk northwest \nwinds of 25 to 35 mph and temperatures lingering in the 50s, except \nin the Upper Valley, will make it feel very much like Autumn. \nMinimum temperatures for Monday night range from the mid 20s to mid \n30s with some upper 30s lingering near Lake Champlain. Winds will \nkeep blowing over most areas, and this should preclude fog or frost. \nThe core of the coldest air will start pulling away Tuesday, but it \nwill be a chilly day in the upper 40s to mid 50s and steady winds \nremaining. Heading into Tuesday night, high pressure will roll in, \nand this should allow winds to slacken and radiative cooling to take \nover. Still, ensemble guidance is relatively stayed with upper 20s \nto upper 30s, and did nudge things a couple degrees towards some \ncolder solutions. There could be some fairly widespread frost \nTuesday night into early Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure will exit east into the Atlantic. This \nwill allow our temperatures to moderate. Over the course of the \nlatter portion of the weak, there will be plenty of northern stream \nsystems that will make their way across the mid-latitude westerlies. \nNone of them are particularly strong, but may offer some reinforcing \nshots of cool air and light rain. So rather seasonable conditions \nare expected. Heading into the extended time frame by next weekend, \na fairly broad ridge will amplify across much of the CONUS east of \nthe Rockies.",
